e>&#x02013;422.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11934740</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><label>33.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Rantanen, V.-V., K. A. Denessiouk, M. Gyllenberg, T. Kosk, and M. S. Johnson. <year>2001</year>. A fragment library based on Gaussian mixtures predicting favorable molecular interactions. <source>J. Mol. Biol.</source> <volume>313</volume>:<fpage>197</fpage>&#x02013;214.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11601856</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><label>34.</label><citation citation-type="other">Press, W. H., S. A. Teukolsky, W. T. Vetterling, and B. P. Flannery. <year>2007</year>. Gaussian mixture models and K-means clustering. <italic>In</italic> Numerical Recipes: The Art of Scientific Computing, 3rd ed. Cambridge University Press, New York. 842&#x02013;850.</citation></ref><ref id="bib35"><label>35.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Goodsell, D. S., and A. J. Olson. <year>2000</year>. Structural symmetry and protein function. <source>Annu. Rev. Biophys. Biomol. Struct.</source> <volume>29</volume>:<fpage>105</fpage>&#x02013;153.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10940245</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib36"><label>36.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Alber, F., M. F. Kim, and A. Sali. <year>2005</year>. Structural characterization of assemblies from overall shape and subcomplex compositions. <source>Structure.</source> <volume>13</volume>:<fpage>435</fpage>&#x02013;445.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15766545</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib37"><label>37.</label><citation citation-type="other">Press, W. H., S. A. Teukolsky, W. T. Vetterling, and B. P. Flannery. <year>2007</year>. Multivariate normal deviates. <italic>In</italic> Numerical Recipes: The Art of Scientific Computing, 3rd ed. Cambridge University Press, New York. 378&#x02013;379.</citation></ref><ref id="bib38"><label>38.</label><citation citation-type="other">Leech, A. R. <year>2001</year>. Molecular Modeling: Principles and Applications. Prentice Hall, Upper Saddle River, NJ. 420&#x02013;422.</citation></ref><ref id="bib39"><label>39.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Lasker, K., O. Dror, M. Shatsky, R. Nussinov, and H. J. Wolfson. <year>2007</year>. EMatch: discovery of high resolution structural homologues of protein domains in intermediate resolution cyro-EM maps. <source>IEEE/ACM Trans. Comput. Biol. Bioinform.</source> <volume>4</volume>:<fpage>28</fpage>&#x02013;39.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17277411</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib40"><label>40.</label><citation citation-type="other">Press, W. H., S. A. Teukolsky, W. T. Vetterling, and B. P. Flannery. <year>2007</year>. Golden section search in one dimension. <italic>In</italic> Numerical Recipes: The Art of Scientific Computing, 3rd ed. Cambridge University Press, New York. 492&#x02013;496.</citation></ref><ref id="bib41"><label>41.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Adman, E. T., J. W. Godden, and S. Turley. <year>1995</year>. The structure of copper-nitrite reductase from <italic>Achromobacter cycloclastes</italic> at five pH values, with NO<sub>2&#x02212;</sub> bound and with type II copper depleted. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>270</volume>:<fpage>27458</fpage>&#x02013;27474.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7499203</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib42"><label>42.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Xu, Z., A. L. Horwich, and P. B. Sigler. <year>1997</year>. The crystal structure of the asymmetric GroEL-ES-(ADP)7 chaperonin complex. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>388</volume>:<fpage>741</fpage>&#x02013;750.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9285585</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib43"><label>43.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Ranson, N. A., G. W. Farr, A. M. Roseman, B. Gowen, W. A. Fenton, A. L. Horwich, and H. R. Saibil. <year>2001</year>. ATP-bound states of GroEL captured by cryo-electron microscopy. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>107</volume>:<fpage>869</fpage>&#x02013;879.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11779463</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib44"><label>44.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Mathieu, M., Y. Modis, J. Ph. Zeelen, C. K. Engel, R. A. Abagyan, A. Ahlberg, B. Rasmussen, V. S. Lamzin, H. Wolf, W. H. Kunau, and R. K. Wierenga. <year>1997</year>. The 1.8 &#x000c5; crystal structure of the dimeric peroxisomal 3-ketoacyl-CoA thiolase of <italic>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</italic>: implications for substrate binding and reaction mechanism. <source>J. Mol. Biol.</source> <volume>273</volume>:<fpage>714</fpage>&#x02013;728.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9402066</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib45"><label>45.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Fita, I., and M. G. Rossmann. <year>1986</year>. The NADPH binding site on beef liver catalase. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>82</volume>:<fpage>1604</fpage>&#x02013;1608.</citation></ref><ref id="bib46"><label>46.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Yu, X., and E. H. Egelman. <year>1997</year>. The RecA hexamer is a structural homologue of ring helicases. <source>Nat. Struct. Biol.</source> <volume>4</volume>:<fpage>101</fpage>&#x02013;104.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9033586</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib47"><label>47.</label><citation citation-type="journal">Grant, J. A., M. A. Gallardo, and B. T. Pickup. <year>1996</year>. A fast method of molecular shape comparison: a simple application of a Gaussian description of molecular shape. <source>J. Comput. Chem.</source> <volume>17</volume>:<fpage>1653</fpage>&#x02013;1666.</citation></ref></ref-list><app-group><app id="app1"><title>APPENDIX</title><sec><title>Force and torque by attractive interaction energy between two distribution functions</title><p>To perform the steepest-descent search method, we must know the force and torque vector of the energy for each subunit. To simplify the problem, we focus on the attractive overlap energy between two distribution functions, <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub> and <italic>f</italic><sub>B</sub>, illustrated in <xref rid="fig12" ref-type="fig">Fig. 12</xref>. We define the attractive fitness energy <italic>E</italic>(<bold>r</bold>) at a point <bold>r</bold> as follow:<disp-formula><tex-math id="M26">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}E({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})=-f_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}).\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><fig position="float" id="fig12"><label>FIGURE 12</label><caption><p>Local force <bold>F</bold><sub>A</sub>(<bold>r</bold>) and a total force <bold>F</bold><sub>A</sub> for a distribution <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub>, by the attractive overlap energy, <italic>E</italic>, of two GMMs, <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub> and <italic>f</italic><sub>B</sub>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="BIO.137125.gs.f12"></graphic></fig><p>The total fitness energy, <italic>E</italic>, is obtained by the integral of <italic>E</italic>(<bold><italic>r</italic></bold>) for the entire space as follow:<disp-formula><tex-math id="M27">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}E={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}E({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}=-{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}f_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}=-ov(f_{{\mathrm{A}}},f_{{\mathrm{B}}}).\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>A local force <bold>F</bold><sub>A</sub>(<bold>r</bold>) for the distribution <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub> at point <bold>r</bold> is defined as the derivative of energy <italic>E</italic>(<bold>r</bold>) by the center position <bold><italic>g</italic></bold><sub>A</sub> of distribution <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub>:<disp-formula><tex-math id="M28">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})=-\frac{{\partial}E({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}=\frac{{\partial}}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}[f_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})].\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>A total force <bold>F</bold><sub>A</sub> for the distribution <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub> at the center point <bold>g</bold><sub>A</sub> is obtained by the integral of <bold>F</bold><sub>A</sub>(<bold>r</bold>) for the entire space:<disp-formula><tex-math id="M29">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}=-\frac{{\partial}E}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}} \\=\frac{{\partial}}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}[ov(f_{{\mathrm{A}}},f_{{\mathrm{B}}})] \\=\frac{{\partial}}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}\left[{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}f_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}\right] \\={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}\frac{{\partial}}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}[f_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})]d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}.\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>A torque around the point <bold>g</bold><sub>A</sub> is described as the integral of the outer product between the positional vector <bold>r</bold> and the local force <bold><italic>F</italic></bold><sub>A</sub>(<bold>r</bold>):<disp-formula><tex-math id="M30">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}-{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}){\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}-{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}{\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}-{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}{\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}={\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}-{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}{\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}},\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where<disp-formula><tex-math id="M31">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p></sec><sec><title>Force and torque of fitness energy for two Gaussian mixture models</title><p>Let us assume that distributions <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub> and <italic>f</italic><sub>B</sub> are described as the GMMs:<disp-formula><tex-math id="M32">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}f_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})={{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\phi}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\vert}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}},{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}) \\f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})={{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bi}}}{\phi}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\vert}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bi}}},{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bi}}}).\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>The centers of gravity, <bold>g</bold><sub>A</sub> and <bold>g</bold><sub>B</sub>, are defined as follows:<disp-formula><tex-math id="M33">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}={{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}} \\{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}={{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bi}}}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{Bi}.\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>Then, the force <bold>F</bold><sub>A</sub> for distribution <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub> is analytically obtained as follows:<disp-formula><tex-math id="M34">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}=-\frac{{\partial}E}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}=\frac{{\partial}}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}[ov(f_{{\mathrm{A}}},f_{{\mathrm{B}}})] \\=\frac{{\partial}}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}\left[{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{j=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}ov({\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}},{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})\right] \\={{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{j=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}\frac{{\partial}}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}}[ov({\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}},{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})] \\=-{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{j=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}ov({\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}},{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})^{-1}({\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}).\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>The partial differential by the center of gravity, <bold>g</bold><sub>A</sub>, is equivalent to the differential by the center of each Gaussian distribution, <bold><italic>&#x003bc;</italic></bold><sub>Ai</sub>, because we assume that each GMM is a rigid body.</p><p>Next, the torque <inline-formula><tex-math id="M35">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> has to be obtained to calculate the torque, <bold>T</bold><sub>A</sub>, for distribution <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub>.<disp-formula><tex-math id="M36">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}=-{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}\frac{{\partial}}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}[f_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})]d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\=-{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}\frac{{\partial}f_{{\mathrm{A}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\=-{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}\frac{{\partial}}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}\left[{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})\right]f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\=-{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}\left[{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}\frac{{\partial}{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})}{{\partial}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}}\right]f_{{\mathrm{B}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}\left[{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}})\right]\left[{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{j=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})\right]d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\={{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{j=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\={{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{j=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}\left[{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\-{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}\left.\right] \\={{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{j=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}\left[{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{r}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}(A_{{\mathrm{i}}},B_{{\mathrm{j}}})-{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mu}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}(A_{{\mathrm{i}}},B_{{\mathrm{j}}})\right],\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where<disp-formula><tex-math id="M37">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{r}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}(A_{{\mathrm{i}}},B_{{\mathrm{j}}})={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula><disp-formula><tex-math id="M38">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mu}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}(A_{{\mathrm{i}}},B_{{\mathrm{j}}})={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\times}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\=\left[{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}\right]{\times}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}).\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>Then, the torque for distribution <italic>f</italic><sub>A</sub> can be described as the sum of the three terms <inline-formula><tex-math id="M39">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{T}}_{{\mathrm{r}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}},{\mathbf{T}}_{{\mu}}^{{\mathrm{O}}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> and <bold>g</bold><sub>A</sub> &#x000d7; <bold>F</bold><sub>A</sub>:<disp-formula><tex-math id="M40">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}={\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}-{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}{\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}} \\={{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}}_{{\mathrm{i=1}}}}}{{\sum^{{\mathrm{N}}_{{\mathrm{B}}}}_{{\mathrm{j=1}}}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\pi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}\left[{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{r}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}(A_{{\mathrm{i}}},B_{{\mathrm{j}}})-{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mu}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}(A_{{\mathrm{i}}},B_{{\mathrm{j}}})\right]-{\mathbf{{\mathrm{g}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}{\times}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{F}}}}_{{\mathrm{A}}}.\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>To calculate <inline-formula><tex-math id="M41">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mu}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> (<italic>A</italic><sub>i</sub><italic>,B</italic><sub>j</sub>), we need the integral<disp-formula><tex-math id="M42">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}=\frac{1}{(2{\pi})^{3}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\vert}^{1/2}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}{\vert}^{1/2}} \\{\times}{\mathrm{exp}}\left[-\frac{1}{2}({\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})^{{\mathrm{T}}} \\{\times}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})^{-1}({\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})\left.\right] \\{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}{\mathrm{exp}}\left[-\frac{1}{2}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}})^{{\mathrm{T}}}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}^{-1})({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}})\right]d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\=\frac{1}{(2{\pi})^{3}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\vert}^{1/2}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}{\vert}^{1/2}}{\mathrm{exp}}\left[-\frac{1}{2}({\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})^{{\mathrm{T}}} \\{\times}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})^{-1}({\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})\left.\right](2{\pi})^{3/2}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}^{-1}{\vert}^{1/2}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}} \\=ov({\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}},{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}){\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}},\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where<disp-formula><tex-math id="M43">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}}=({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}^{-1})^{-1}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}^{-1}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}).\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>Then, the torque <inline-formula><tex-math id="M44">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mu}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}(A_{{\mathrm{i}}},B_{{\mathrm{j}}})\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> is described as<disp-formula><tex-math id="M45">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mu}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}(A_{{\mathrm{i}}},B_{{\mathrm{j}}})=ov({\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}},{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}){\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}}{\times}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}).\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>Calculation of the term <inline-formula><tex-math id="M46">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{T}}}}_{{\mathrm{r}}}^{{\mathrm{O}}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> is more complicated. First, we obtain the second-moment matrix <italic>Q</italic> for the product of <italic>&#x003c6;</italic><sub>A<italic>&#x003b9;</italic></sub>(<bold>r</bold>) and <italic>&#x003c6;</italic><sub>Bj</sub>(<bold>r</bold>):<disp-formula><tex-math id="M47">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}Q={\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{rr}}}}^{{\mathrm{T}}}{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}){\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}})d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}=\frac{1}{(2{\pi})^{3}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\vert}^{1/2}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}{\vert}^{1/2}} \\{\times}{\mathrm{exp}}\left[-\frac{1}{2}({\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})^{{\mathrm{T}}}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})^{-1}({\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})\right] \\{\int _{-{\infty}}^{{\infty}}}{\mathbf{{\mathrm{rr}}}}^{{\mathrm{T}}}{\mathrm{exp}}\left[-\frac{1}{2}({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}})^{{\mathrm{T}}}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}^{-1})({\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}})\right]d{\mathbf{{\mathrm{r}}}} \\=\frac{1}{(2{\pi})^{3}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}{\vert}^{1/2}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}{\vert}^{1/2}}{\mathrm{exp}}\left[-\frac{1}{2}({\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})^{{\mathrm{T}}}({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})^{-1} \\({\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}-{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})\left.\right](2{\pi})^{3/2}{\vert}{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}^{-1}{\vert}^{1/2} \\{\times}\left[({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}^{-1})^{-1}+{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}}^{{\mathrm{T}}}\right] \\=ov({\phi}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}},{\phi}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}})\left[({\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Ai}}}^{-1}+{\Sigma}_{{\mathrm{Bj}}}^{-1})^{-1}+{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}}{\mathbf{{\mu}}}_{{\mathrm{AiBj}}}^{{\mathrm{T}}}\right].\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>Using matrix <italic>Q</italic>, the term <inline-formula><tex-math id="M48">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\u